  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis reveals a strikingly clear signal: **Tryptophan metabolism (KEGG map00380)** is the dominant affected pathway, with FDR = 0.0 and a fold-enrichment of 583.8 across 54 pathway members. Seven of the twelve input metabolites map to this pathway — **L-Tryptophan (C00078)**, **Serotonin (C00780)**, **Melatonin (C01598)**, **Tryptamine (C00398)**, **Indoleacetaldehyde (C00637)**, **3-Hydroxyanthranilic acid (C00632)**, and **Quinolinic acid (C03722)** — all confirmed by direct pathway membership lookup. A secondary enriched pathway, **Biogenic amine synthesis (Wikipathways WP550)**, captures acetylcholine (C01996) alongside the same tryptophan-derived neuromodulators.\n\n**Verified driver compounds**: L-Tryptophan is the canonical entry point, feeding into multiple downstream arms of the pathway. KEGG reaction-pathway analysis confirms that L-Tryptophan is **upstream of Serotonin** (C00078 → C00643 → C00780, 2 hops; bidirectional traversal). Serotonin is in turn **upstream of Melatonin** (C00780 → C00978 → C01598, 2 hops, forward direction), establishing a verified L-Tryptophan → Serotonin → Melatonin axis. Quinolinic acid occupies the kynurenine branch of tryptophan catabolism leading to NAD⁺ biosynthesis; its presence alongside 3-hydroxyanthranilic acid suggests concurrent activation of the neurotoxic tryptophan–kynurenine–quinolinic acid cascade.\n\n**Biological significance**: The coordinated perturbation across tryptophan metabolism points to a treatment-induced reprogramming of tryptophan flux along its major branches — the serotonin/melatonin neuroendocrine axis and the kynurenine/quinolinic acid immunometabolic arm. Elevated quinolinic acid is a recognized marker of neuroinflammation and excitotoxicity, while shifts in acetylcholine suggest cholinergic signaling alterations. The concurrent changes in melatonin and serotonin indicate broad disruption of the tryptophan-derived amine network with implications for mood, circadian regulation, and immune tolerance. This pattern is consistent with exposure to a xenobiotic such as aflatoxin B1 (C19586, also in the input list), where the liver activates xenobiotic metabolism while mounting an inflammatory tryptophan-shunt response.",
